<section class="section" style="padding-top:32px;">
  <div class="container-narrow prose">
    <div class="eyebrow eyebrow-muted">The capability</div>
    <h2>One agent, one desk, one job</h2>
    <p>Most AI gets sold as a platform: here is the tool, here are the seats, good luck. That works when everyone's problem is the same shape. It does not work for a billing desk with its own payer rules, a job site with its own paperwork, or a sales team with its own definition of a good account.</p>
    <p>So the product works the other way around. A job description goes in. A working agent comes back, connected to the systems you already use. Six are running right now, doing six unrelated jobs, and none would be much use at the other five. The capability is what we sell: not those six agents, but the ability to produce the seventh for whatever your team is missing.</p>
    <p><a href="/agents/">See what each one hands a human &rarr;</a></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section-hairline section-soft">
  <div class="container-narrow prose">
    <div class="eyebrow eyebrow-muted">What we believe</div>
    <h2>How we think about it</h2>
    <ul class="checklist" style="margin-top:24px;">
      <li>An agent is worth generating only where it earns its keep: more revenue, less wasted time, better decisions. Everywhere else the answer is to skip it.</li>
      <li>Judgment does not come from a model. It comes from people who understand the work and have looked hard at the evidence.</li>
      <li>We take no vendor fees, so the connectors we pick are the ones that fit your stack, not the ones that pay us.</li>
      <li>Proof beats adjectives. We would rather show you an agent that runs than tell you how good we are.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section-hairline">
  <div class="container-narrow prose">
    <div class="eyebrow eyebrow-muted">Who is who</div>
    <h2>Nadia and Logan are agents, not people</h2>
    <p>This matters when you are buying judgment, so we put it in writing. <strong>Every spec, recommendation and deliverable that reaches you is owned and reviewed by a human.</strong> What lets a team our size move fast is that we run our own agents for the parts that do not need judgment: monitoring, first-pass research, drafting, and formatting.</p>

    <div class="note-card">
      <p><strong>Nadia</strong> is an AI agent, not a person. She is our chief of staff and the byline you will see on <i>The Briefing</i>. She watches the sources we cover, pulls first-pass research, and drafts. A human reviews everything before it goes out.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="note-card">
      <p><strong>Logan</strong> is an AI agent, not a person. Logan runs program operations: status, scheduling, and follow-through on every build. Logan keeps work moving. A human decides what moves.</p>
    </div>

    <p>The same honesty applies to anything we build for you. If an agent drafted it, we will say an agent drafted it. If a person signed off, we will say who. You should expect that from anyone selling you this, and you rarely get it.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section-hairline section-soft">
